        <h2>Legacy and Historical Assessment</h2><p><br>Despite persistent controversies, Khamenei remained the dominant force in Iranian politics for nearly four decades. His supporters regarded him as a steadfast defender of Islamic governance, national sovereignty, and resistance to foreign intervention. His critics, by contrast, accused his administration of authoritarianism, systematic political repression, and severe restrictions on civil liberties. His leadership thus remained one of the most contested subjects in contemporary Middle Eastern political discourse.</p>

    
    
    
        <p><br>Ultimately, Ali Khamenei's political career left an indelible mark on the Islamic Republic of Iran. His decades-long stewardship shaped the country's ideological foundations, political institutions, and regional posture. His policies fundamentally influenced Iran's relationships with global powers and neighboring states. His death in 2026 — during military strikes in Tehran — brought an end to a long and complex era in Iranian political history. Yet the legacy of his leadership continues to shape Iran's political trajectory and its standing in international affairs.</p>

    
    
    
    




